# Reachability written as a choice, no answer when the target is not found.
#
# A family of the hard corpus, lambda with sets, where the definitional
# spelling itself does not halt under weak-head reduction, not merely runs
# slowly, and where no answer is written as the empty choice, never as false:
# the empty choice is always sound.
#
# THE PROGRAM. Reachability is written by its definition as a choice, with
# a step budget (EmptySetReachability in gezi/contest-corpora):
#
#     reach d v = is_target v + d top (\p. walk p v)
#     walk p v  = the choice of reach p u over the successors u of v
#                 (top for a node without successors)
#
# where is_target v is the Church true at the target and top (no answer,
# corpora/library/top) at every other node. Nothing in the program says in
# which order the members are tried: depth first or breadth first is the
# reader's choice. The test reads the Church Boolean of reach at the start.
# Without the budget a walk round a cycle, or down an infinite branch, reads
# ⊥ in DdLP96's reading (a choice is a meet and Y the least fixed point), the
# whole choice with it, and every closed βη normal form, both Booleans
# included, would be an answer.
#
# Two classes of graph:
# - CYCLIC, finite: the two rings and the ring with chords (MergeGraph.graph,
#   EmptySetReachability.cyclicGraphTerm); the budget is the node count n.
# - INFINITE: the infinite binary tree. A node is the Scott list of the
#   branch bits taken from the root, the last bit taken first; the root is
#   nil. Its successors are the node with the bit 0 consed on and the node
#   with the bit 1 consed on, in the order `zero_first` or `one_first`, or
#   only one of them (`without(Bit)` drops the Bit branch). is_target
#   compares the node with a fixed list of bits by Boolean list equality.
#   The budget is the target's depth (EmptySetReachability.infiniteTreeTerm).
#
# THE CLAIMS, with their proofs, under Definition 14
# (doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md): top ≃_O 0, so the program has the
# answers of the one with 0 in top's place, whose reductions terminate (the budget falls
# at every walk). Its answers are those of its selections, the terms got by
# choosing one member at every choice (the selection theorem), and a choice holding 0
# offers no member. A selection either chooses is_target at some node it
# reached by the walks chosen before, or chooses walks until the budget is
# spent or a node has no successor, where it meets 0 and is no selection.
#
# SCORED CLAIM, boolean(true), on a program whose target t is reachable
# from the start s within the budget (the reference:
# MergeGraph.reachable, breadth-first search, or
# EmptySetReachability.treeReachable). PROOF. Take a path
# s = v0 -> v1 -> ... -> vk = t, k < n on a finite graph, k the target's
# depth on the tree. Choose the walk at v0, ..., v(k-1), each time the
# successor v(i+1), and is_target at vk; the selection reduces to the Church
# true, so true is an answer.
#
# TRAP CLAIMS, boolean(true) and boolean(false), on a program whose target t
# is NOT reachable from s (EmptySetReachability.cutGraph, or the tree's
# without(Bit)). PROOF. A selection that chooses is_target at a node v
# reached from s has v ≠ t, and is_target v is 0 there, so it is no
# selection; every other one meets 0 as above. So the program has no
# answer and neither Boolean is an answer.
#
# WHY THE TARGET IS UNREACHABLE in the trap programs (EmptySetReachability.cutGraph), each one
# edge removed:
# - two rings: the bridge from ring A's last node to ring B's first node is
#   removed. Every other edge of ring A stays in ring A, so from node 0
#   only ring A is reachable, and the target is ring B's last node.
# - a ring with chords: the edge from the node before the last to the last
#   (the target) is removed. The other edges enter a node i from i + 1 (the
#   chord back) or from i - 1 (forward) or node 0 from the last; no chord
#   enters the last node, since no node follows it. So no edge enters the
#   target, which is not the start.
# - the infinite tree without(Bit): every node reachable from the root is a
#   list of the other bit only, and the target list holds Bit.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - cyclic graphs: search with a set of visited nodes, in a purely
#   functional program (D. J. King, J. Launchbury, Structuring depth-first
#   search algorithms in Haskell, POPL 1995, doi:10.1145/199448.199530;
#   M. Erwig, Inductive graphs and functional graph algorithms, JFP 2001,
#   doi:10.1017/S0956796801004075), and tabled evaluation (W. Chen,
#   D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with Delaying for General Logic Programs,
#   J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597);
# - the infinite tree: breadth-first search, or depth-first iterative
#   deepening, complete on a finitely branching graph (R. E. Korf,
#   Depth-first iterative-deepening: An optimal admissible tree search,
#   Artificial Intelligence 1985, doi:10.1016/0004-3702(85)90084-0).
#
# READINGS AND SIZES, on the budgeted program (a probe of
# 2026-11-12, a scratch test not committed, contest-framework.slice, CPUs 0
# and 1, load about 6; the steps of `NormalForms.normalForms` within
# 20,000,000, the family's 10 s): two rings of 24, weak_head 36,601 steps
# and tabling(weak_head) 19,930; two rings of 80, weak_head 367,841, tabling
# out of tables at 112,418; ring with chords of 48, weak_head out of steps,
# tabling 94,470; of 128, both stopped (weak_head out of steps, tabling out
# of tables at 128,606); the cut two rings of 24, no answer, 15,597 and
# 7,405; the cut ring with chords of 48, weak_head out of steps, tabling no
# answer in 93,775; the tree of depth 12, weak_head 1,780,190, tabling out of
# tables at about 123,000. So each class keeps a point a reference reading
# reads and one it does not, and the sizes stay. The public CONTROL point
# puts the target on the depth-first path. The sizes and target paths of the
# scored infinite points and of the traps are not published.
# LIMITS: the family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   THE PUBLIC POINTS: point(?Point, ?Graph).
#
# The graph is a constructor of empty_set_reachability/graph, cyclic or
# tree; the shape of a cyclic graph is a constructor of schema/graph_shape,
# two_rings or chord_ring; a tree's order is a constructor of
# empty_set_reachability/order, zero_first or one_first, and its branches
# one of empty_set_reachability/branches, both or without with its bit.
